WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.770
A question I get asked all the time is
"Luca how can you memorize so many words

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.050
across so many languages?" And my simple
answer is: "I build a word spider web", and

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:14.309
in this video, I'm going to give you the
tools to create it, to maintain it, and to

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:23.189
make it stronger. If I say the word "game"
what is the very first thing that comes

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.650
to your mind?
Probably something associated with

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.210
"play" - playing games - but the reality is
that "game" has many other meanings.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.770
For example, you can say "I was hunting game", 
meaning "I was hunting wild animals" or

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.000
"Are you game?" - like "are you down?,  do you want to do something?". So this goes to

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.440
show that many words have multiple
meanings and functions according to

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.000
their context and what gives them meaning? Context, that is, surrounding words.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.110
So, whenever you find a word that you don't
know in a foreign language, always make

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:56.430
sure that you either take the sentence
in which that word is embedded and you

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:01.050
copy it somewhere on your notebook, for
example, or you create your own sentence

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.070
in which you embed that word. A very
common mistake is for example to take

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:09.510
all these words that you don't know from
a text and simply put it on a list. But

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:14.100
isolated words are not as strong as
words in context, embedded in sentences.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.810
Always remember that as a first rule.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:26.370
A simple yet very effective way to
memorize and learn new words is to

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:32.430
listen while reading. Think about it for
a second: you can for example read a text

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.410
as a beginner material or you can have
podcasts with scripts or movies with

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.160
subtitles. The most important thing is
that you listen while reading or reading

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.000
while listening. If you just listen to
the language without subtitles or a

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.470
script, you will not see the written word,
you will probably not understand if you

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:53.369
don't have a good level. On the other
hand, if you just read a text, you will

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:57.600
not see and hear how the language is
spoken, so you will miss out on the

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:05.759
spoken language. The easiest solution? Read
while listening as much as you can.

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:09.000
When you read a text in a foreign language
there's a very high chance that you're

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.000
going to stumble upon a lot of words
that you don't know. A very common

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.730
reaction is to try to strike and
underline and look up every single word

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.209
that appears on the text. I don't think
that this is an efficient way to deal

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:25.350
with it. A much more efficient way is to
focus just on the words that are

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.579
RELEVANT for you. Let me give you an
example: if you're an engineer, words such

00:02:29.579 --> 00:02:33.959
as "tile" or "pillar" will probably be much
more interesting and relevant to you

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:39.120
than if you were a doctor for example,
and for a doctor some other words might

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.459
be more interesting, and anyway, the brain
discards a lot of words that it deems

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:49.590
not important. So just focus on ONE THIRD
of the unknown words that you find in

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:56.239
the text because the brain will remember
those words if they're relevant for you.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:06.070
Heart, elephant and sand. If you think
about it, these simple words have a

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:11.290
constellation of elements around them.
For example you can say "(see) pink elephants"

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.060
or "the elephant in the room", "to draw a
line in the sand", "at the heart of the matter"

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.480
"golden heart", and so on and so forth.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:25.540
This goes to show that the brain creates big
networks of associated elements and this

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:30.430
is how you can use your brain in order
to start learning new words. Every new

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.780
piece of information you can link it to
old pieces of information, so always make

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.680
sure that you look at words from this
perspective and think "how can I

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.880
associate this new piece of information
to the other pieces of information?" in

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.980
the network that I have talked about. And
this is the "word spiderweb" I was talking

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.000
about at the beginning of this video.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:58.459
Some languages have notoriously long
words. Let's take the German word

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:03.739
"Freundschaftsbezeigung" which means "display of
friendship" but if you think about it, the

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:07.550
first reaction is to get scared by he
length of this word, but then when

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.910
looking closely, you will realize that
this word is made of "Freundschaft" -

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:15.170
- friendship -  and "Bezeigung" - 
or "Bezeigungen" - display, displays of".

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:20.209
This makes the word easier to retain,
easier to pronounce, and easier to

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:25.430
remember. So get into the habit of
looking at words as made of smaller

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:30.380
parts. If you learn how these smaller
parts make longer words, things will get

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.200
much easier in terms of everything.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:40.940
Romans used to say "Repetita juvant", which means "repeating helps" and that's

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.360
true, and that's what we do at college
university, at school: we tend to repeat

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.410
things over and over, but repeating
things in the very same way in which we

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:52.639
had been exposed to the very first time
is not actually a very efficient way of

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:55.970
dealing with texts. Let me give you a
metaphor: imagine that you have to

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:59.930
conquer a castle - that is, conquering the
language - if you always attack it through

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:03.860
the main door, the defendants will
realize your tactics and your strategy

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:07.610
and it will be easier to defend the
castle. If, instead, you attack the castle

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:12.320
from multiple angles, you are more likely
to win the battle. To give you a very

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.849
concrete example, if you listen while
reading the text the very first time that

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:21.229
you got exposed to it, three or four days
down the line, do things differently

00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:25.340
maybe just listen to the text, or just
read it, or listen to it in the target

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.650
language while reading it in your own
native language.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:32.479
Whatever is different is relevant and
it makes a big difference in the way our

00:05:32.479 --> 00:05:37.280
brain retains information because we're
adding VARIETY to it. Novelty and

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:44.680
repetition is a fantastic combination to
retain words, texts, and whatever you want.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.540
"Verba volant sed scripta manent",  "spoken words fly while written words stay"

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:57.850
another wise quote from the wise Romans. Why is this so relevant 2,000 years ago

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:02.320
as it is now? It's because words that are
spoken, they just fly around the air and you

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.000
need a system in place to catch them. So
always make sure that you have a device,

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.740
a note-taking device - be it your mobile
phone or your notebook - to catch these

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.610
words and these sentences on the fly. You
can do it everywhere. You can do it while

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.460
you're sitting and you're trying to
learn the language, and you can do it

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.150
while you're talking to your friends in
a bar, you can do it even while walking

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.390
and you come up with a word that you
want to know the meaning of in a

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:28.960
foreign language. Whatever it is, just
make sure that you have a notebook that

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.990
you can fit in your pocket
and you can carry it with you at all

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:37.660
times. And another tip is when you mark
words or sentences, always make sure that

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:42.160
you mark the PLACE and the TIME where
this happened, because it's going to give

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:47.050
you a very clear reference in time and
space for your episodic memory. It makes

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:52.470
a huge difference if you get into the
habit of doing this every single day.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.760
This video contains a lot of information
and I've condensed it for the sake of

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.840
brevity and clarity. If you want to know
more, you can click on the link in the

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.620
description box below and you will be
able to read a very extensive article

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.790
about this very same topic. So here you
have it. These are the seven tools to

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:13.870
create and strengthen your "word
spider web". All you need to do now is to

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:17.500
get out there and catch as many "word
flies" as possible.

